WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.933 --> 00:00:05.897
- So what happened next in
terms of reconciliation?

00:00:06.157 --> 00:00:09.557
- Well, Lincoln got assassinated
just a few days after-

00:00:09.757 --> 00:00:11.337
... Lee's troops laid down their arms.

00:00:11.497 --> 00:00:16.327
- Can you talk to the assassination? He was
shot five days after the surrender, right?

00:00:16.377 --> 00:00:17.017
- Yes, he was.

00:00:17.437 --> 00:00:20.217
- So who was Booth? Who
was John Wilkes Booth?

00:00:21.237 --> 00:00:24.797
Why did he assassinate Lincoln,
and what impact did that have?

00:00:24.887 --> 00:00:26.717
- Well, he was the youngest

00:00:27.537 --> 00:00:31.437
of the most famous acting family in
the United States. Junius Brutus

00:00:31.617 --> 00:00:35.247
Booth and Edwin Booth, his father
and older brother, were very famous

00:00:35.397 --> 00:00:39.357
actors, and he was a very successful actor,
too, John Wilkes Booth was. He was good at

00:00:39.417 --> 00:00:42.397
jumping around the stage,
and he was very athletic.

00:00:42.977 --> 00:00:47.067
Um, and an ardent pro-Confederate.

00:00:47.097 --> 00:00:48.527
- That's the more important- ... aspect

00:00:48.537 --> 00:00:51.677
- Not ardent, not ardent enough to put on a

00:00:51.777 --> 00:00:56.027
uniform for the Confederacy,
but ardent enough to, you know-

00:00:56.137 --> 00:01:00.117
... argue for the Confederacy. He
claims ... I mean, not claims. He,

00:01:00.277 --> 00:01:03.937
he heard Lincoln make a statement about
maybe Black soldiers getting the vote,

00:01:04.477 --> 00:01:08.377
and he said that was it for him, he ...
that somebody needed to kill the tyrant.

00:01:08.997 --> 00:01:11.317
This is very ... This is
after Lee had surrendered.

00:01:11.557 --> 00:01:14.437
- So he was on the Union side.

00:01:14.577 --> 00:01:15.557
- Oh, yeah. Oh, yes, he-

00:01:15.597 --> 00:01:18.757
- But Lincoln was a tyrant in his eyes.

00:01:18.957 --> 00:01:22.637
- Lincoln was a tyrant because of his sta-
because of his treatment of the white

00:01:22.697 --> 00:01:27.757
South. And he had plotted earlier
to kidnap Lincoln and hold him for

00:01:27.837 --> 00:01:31.757
ransom, and that didn't work out.
So then he got this little group of

00:01:31.817 --> 00:01:35.547
losers around him to help. I
mean, real misfits. Their,

00:01:35.657 --> 00:01:39.577
their plan was to kill Seward,
Andrew Johnson. They were gonna

00:01:39.997 --> 00:01:42.777
cut off the top leadership
of the United States.

00:01:42.897 --> 00:01:42.977
- Yep.

00:01:43.177 --> 00:01:46.517
- Kill the vice, kill the president, kill the
vice president, kill the secretary of state.

00:01:46.787 --> 00:01:48.137
That's what they wanted to do.

00:01:48.177 --> 00:01:50.497
- On the Johnson side, they chickened out.

00:01:50.577 --> 00:01:54.506
- That guy chickened out. A guy named Lewis
Powell was sent to kill Seward. Seward had

00:01:54.517 --> 00:01:57.577
been in an accident and had
his neck in a big brace,

00:01:58.517 --> 00:02:02.326
and he slashed at Seward, huge
scars on the side of his face,

00:02:02.417 --> 00:02:06.237
but the knife hit the brace and didn't
kill Seward. So Seward was disfigured. His

00:02:06.317 --> 00:02:07.077
son was wounded.

00:02:08.107 --> 00:02:12.357
The guy who was supposed to kill Johnson
chickened out, and Booth killed Lincoln.

00:02:12.717 --> 00:02:16.537
And there was a manhunt for Lincoln,
or for Booth. It took a while, and he

00:02:16.597 --> 00:02:20.117
was shot in a barn rather than
captured and put on trial.

00:02:21.057 --> 00:02:24.947
But four of the conspirators were
put on trial, including one woman, a

00:02:24.957 --> 00:02:28.857
woman named Mary Surratt, who ran a boarding house
where some of them stayed, and they were all

00:02:28.917 --> 00:02:34.576
hanged at Old Capitol Prison.
And the combination of th-

00:02:34.697 --> 00:02:38.397
that hanging, they were tried
by a military commission and

00:02:38.457 --> 00:02:42.447
hanged, but they also put on trial
and hanged the commandant of

00:02:42.517 --> 00:02:46.337
Andersonville Prison, which was the most
infamous of the Confederate prison of war

00:02:46.457 --> 00:02:50.267
camps. They hanged him as
well. And it seemed that

00:02:50.297 --> 00:02:54.197
those hangings sort of took
the edge, the sort of we

00:02:54.237 --> 00:02:58.147
need some retribution edge off
attitudes in the North. I think

00:02:58.197 --> 00:03:02.807
those things helped offset Lincoln's
assassination in a sort of odd way

00:03:03.697 --> 00:03:07.477
because the temperature
went down after that.

00:03:07.957 --> 00:03:10.487
- You talk about, as maybe
a bit of an aside,

00:03:12.077 --> 00:03:17.657
But that we Americans like to spin up
conspiracy theories about assassinations-

00:03:17.737 --> 00:03:18.017
- We do

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:23.777
- ... of our presidents. So that's certainly
very true, maybe truer than most about

00:03:23.817 --> 00:03:25.017
Lincoln's assassination.

00:03:25.697 --> 00:03:29.497
- There was a long literature actually.
It began with a chemist named

00:03:29.577 --> 00:03:35.117
Otto Eisenschiml many, many decades ago
that implicated Secretary of War Stanton-

00:03:35.177 --> 00:03:38.837
... and blamed the radicals were
unhappy with Lincoln for not being

00:03:38.997 --> 00:03:42.857
harsher on the former Confederates,
and what they wanted to do was

00:03:43.117 --> 00:03:46.867
get him out of the way. They thought they
could manipulate Andrew Johnson if they got

00:03:46.937 --> 00:03:50.797
Andrew Johnson in charge. So they,

00:03:51.037 --> 00:03:54.877
then they would pose these questions. How
come no one was guarding Lincoln that night?

00:03:54.917 --> 00:03:58.557
How come the only bridge over the Potomac
that wasn't guarded is the one that Booth

00:03:58.617 --> 00:04:00.537
escaped across? All of these.

00:04:01.717 --> 00:04:05.637
How come Grant didn't go to the play
with Lincoln? The Lincolns asked them.

00:04:05.697 --> 00:04:09.577
Well, they didn't 'cause Mrs. Lincoln didn't like...
I mean, the, Mrs. Grant didn't like Mrs. Lincoln.

00:04:09.677 --> 00:04:13.397
I mean, you, there are answers to all the
questions. But they put all these together,

00:04:13.577 --> 00:04:17.327
added two and two and got
five, and said that it was,

00:04:17.617 --> 00:04:21.487
it was a plot on the part of the radical
wing of the Republican Party to get rid of

00:04:21.517 --> 00:04:24.737
Lincoln 'cause Lincoln didn't wanna
be harsh enough on the South.

00:04:25.557 --> 00:04:28.977
- Yeah, it's difficult to
believe that dramatic,

00:04:29.657 --> 00:04:31.987
sometimes catastrophic things happen for

00:04:32.117 --> 00:04:36.617
seemingly... That, that like
the random, as you said, loser

00:04:37.517 --> 00:04:41.467
- It's not satisfying. You want a more
satisfying, and that is a big conspiracy

00:04:41.497 --> 00:04:45.387
is better. There are all these forces at
work to get rid of Lincoln, not just that

00:04:45.437 --> 00:04:49.017
the one guy assigned to guard
Lincoln, there's no Secret Service

00:04:49.077 --> 00:04:52.637
then, went across the
street to get a drink.

00:04:52.777 --> 00:04:56.617
And Booth had played in that theater many
times. He knew exactly the layout. He'd

00:04:56.677 --> 00:05:00.037
come in earlier. He'd arrange... I mean,
it, it just, that's the way it worked out.

00:05:00.317 --> 00:05:05.437
- And I have to say, it, it's kinda interesting.
I have to be honest with my own mind

00:05:06.397 --> 00:05:09.096
that, like, I love a good
conspiracy. My brain does.

00:05:09.517 --> 00:05:10.817
- Every, almost everybody does.

00:05:10.857 --> 00:05:14.837
- I think it's, what is it?
It, it's maybe depressing or

00:05:14.937 --> 00:05:20.717
heartbreaking to know that so much
destruction can be caused by random events.

00:05:22.117 --> 00:05:25.797
You want there to be a larger

00:05:28.237 --> 00:05:35.077
system at play that it makes
you feel like there's a meaning

00:05:35.136 --> 00:05:41.637
to this whole thing as opposed to the tides
of history can turn on a stupid actor

00:05:41.997 --> 00:05:46.157
shooting one of the greatest, if not
the greatest president in the history

00:05:46.167 --> 00:05:49.327
- That's correct. Killing
him, jumping out of

00:05:49.377 --> 00:05:53.237
the presidential box, breaking
his leg when he hit the stage,

00:05:53.437 --> 00:05:55.897
limping off, and getting away. Yep.

00:05:56.057 --> 00:05:59.957
- Over 700,000 people dead, these epic

00:06:00.077 --> 00:06:04.917
battles, all of that. Lincoln
survives all of it, and then there's

00:06:06.177 --> 00:06:10.147
- When they had the grand review in Washington,
when Sherman's armies and the Army of

00:06:10.177 --> 00:06:13.597
the Potomac marched over
two days, this amazing

00:06:13.637 --> 00:06:17.517
display of the troops that had won the war,
and troops who had run the war, one of the

00:06:17.597 --> 00:06:21.497
most common comments was, "Lincoln's
not here. Of all people who

00:06:21.537 --> 00:06:24.857
should've been here, Lincoln should've been
here, and he doesn't get to watch this.

00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:27.077
He's not in the presidential
box watching this."

00:06:28.177 --> 00:06:29.597
And the soldiers talked about it too.

00:06:30.877 --> 00:06:34.637
- It's heartbreaking, really, especially
I would've loved to see his evolution

00:06:35.157 --> 00:06:43.107
as a leader, as a human being, and the
words he may have, he may have found

00:06:44.277 --> 00:06:48.397
to direct us as a country, you know,
in the, in the Reconstruction.

00:06:48.617 --> 00:06:52.117
- Yep. Would've been different.
We can't tell how different.

00:06:52.157 --> 00:06:56.437
But it would've been different, and I
think it would've worked more smoothly.

